## 5. MuleSoft MCP URL Resolution Algorithm

For each environment the user selected, resolve the public base URL using a strict **three-tier fallback**: MCP → ASK user → placeholder. Never silently fail.

### 5.1 Tier 1 — MCP

Call `list_applications` first (the canonical CloudHub source):

```
CallMcpTool: list_applications
  server: user-mulesoft
  arguments:
    environmentName: <ENV>     // e.g., "DEV"
    appName: <api-name>        // e.g., "sfmc-consents-sys-api"
```

Defensively read the response — the MCP tool descriptors document only inputs, so access fields with optional chaining and try multiple shapes:

```
fullDomain =
     application?.fullDomain
  || target?.deploymentSettings?.http?.inbound?.publicUrl
  || data?.[0]?.fullDomain
  || data?.[0]?.application?.fullDomain
```

If `fullDomain` resolves, the base URL is `https://{fullDomain}` (CloudHub default scheme).

If `list_applications` returned nothing or no domain field could be extracted, fall back to:

```
CallMcpTool: list_api_instances
  server: user-mulesoft
  arguments:
    environmentName: <ENV>
```

Match an instance whose `assetId` / `instanceLabel` / `name` corresponds to the API and read `consumerEndpoint` (or equivalent URL field).

### 5.2 Tier 2 — ASK user

If MCP resolution fails for any env, you **must** ask the user (do not silently move to a placeholder). Use `AskQuestion` with one prompt per unresolved env, batched into a single question form. Example prompt:

> "MCP could not resolve a public URL for `QA`. Paste the base URL (e.g., `https://my-api-qa-xxxx.usa-e1.cloudhub.io`), or choose 'Skip' to use a TODO placeholder."

### 5.3 Tier 3 — Placeholder (last resort)

Only if the user skips or provides no value, set the collection variable to a literal placeholder:

```
{ "key": "base_url_<ENV>", "value": "TODO-set-base-url-for-<ENV>" }
```

Surface a single chat warning listing every env that fell through to placeholder, e.g., `"WARNING: base_url_QA and base_url_UAT were not resolved — set them in the Variables tab before running."`.

### 5.4 Decision Diagram

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Start[For each selected env] --> Call1["list_applications<br/>environmentName=ENV<br/>appName=API"]
    Call1 --> Found{Has fullDomain?}
    Found -->|Yes| Extract["base_url_ENV<br/>= https://{fullDomain}"]
    Found -->|No| Call2["list_api_instances<br/>environmentName=ENV"]
    Call2 --> Match{Match by label/asset?}
    Match -->|Yes| Extract2[Read consumerEndpoint]
    Match -->|No| AskUser["AskQuestion: paste base URL<br/>for ENV (or skip)"]
    AskUser --> Provided{User provided?}
    Provided -->|Yes| UseUserUrl[Use user-supplied URL]
    Provided -->|Skipped| Placeholder["base_url_ENV<br/>= 'TODO-set-base-url-for-ENV'"]
    Extract --> Variable[Set collection variable]
    Extract2 --> Variable
    UseUserUrl --> Variable
    Placeholder --> Variable
```

### 5.5 Notes

- No `list_environments` tool exists in the MuleSoft MCP — env names are user-supplied constants from §3.
- The `local` env always uses `http://0.0.0.0:8081` (or `http://localhost:8081`) — do **not** call MCP for it.

## 6. MUnit Data Extraction Algorithm

When the user picks "Derive from MUnit", follow this exact procedure.

### 6.1 Step 1 — Glob suites

Glob `src/test/munit/*-test-suite.xml`. Process each suite file in turn.

### 6.2 Step 2 — Pre-filter non-HTTP-triggered flows (CRITICAL)

Inspect the suite's `<munit:set-event>` attributes **and** look up the source flow it tests in `src/main/mule/*.xml`. If the flow's source is **not** an HTTP listener — i.e., it is `anypoint-mq:subscriber`, `scheduler`, `file:listener`, `jms:listener`, `vm:listener`, or any other event-driven source — **skip the entire suite**. These flows have no HTTP entry point and therefore no Postman equivalent.

**Detection signals (from the `<munit:set-event>` attributes shape, per `gen-munit-implementation.mdc` §"Message Source Attribute Structures"):**

| Attribute shape | Source | Action |
|---|---|---|
| `headers`, `queryParams`, `uriParams`, `requestPath`, `method` | `http:listener` | **Include** |
| `properties.correlationId`, `messageId`, `contentType` | `anypoint-mq:subscriber` | **Skip** |
| Bare payload only, no attributes | `scheduler` | **Skip** |
| `path`, `filename`, `size`, `timestamp` | `file:listener` | **Skip** |
| JMS / VM / custom message attributes | `jms:listener`, `vm:listener`, etc. | **Skip** |

**Cross-check:** open the source flow XML referenced by the suite (usually `<flow-ref>` to `{api}-main` or a named flow) and confirm the source element. If you find `<anypoint-mq:subscriber>`, `<scheduler>`, `<file:listener>`, `<jms:listener>`, `<vm:listener>`, etc., skip the suite.

**Reporting:** emit a single chat note after processing all suites listing every skipped suite and the reason, e.g.:

```
Skipped non-HTTP suites:
- order-mq-consumer-test-suite.xml — flow source is anypoint-mq:subscriber
- nightly-batch-test-suite.xml — flow source is scheduler
```

### 6.3 Step 3 — Classify tests

Parse each `<munit:test name="...">` in the surviving suites and classify by name pattern (per `gen-munit-implementation.mdc` §"File Naming Convention"):

- `*-success*` / `*-positive*` / `*-happy*` → **happy-path bucket**
- `*-error*` / `*-validation-error*` / `*-not-found*` / `*-unauthorized*` / `*-server-error*` / `*-bad-request*` → **negative bucket**

Map the scenario keyword to an HTTP status using **this fixed allow-list table**. Codes outside this list are dropped:

| Scenario keyword in test name | HTTP status emitted |
|---|---|
| `validation-error`, `bad-request`, `invalid` | `400` |
| `unauthorized`, `auth-error`, `missing-credentials` | `401` |
| `not-found`, `missing` | `404` |
| `server-error`, `internal-error`, `unhandled-error` | `500` |
| anything else (e.g., `forbidden`, `duplicate`, `unprocessable`, `conflict`, connectivity) | **DROP — do not emit** |

### 6.4 Step 4 — Extract request shape from `<munit:set-event>`

Inside `<munit:execution>` find the first `<munit:set-event>`. From `attributes` extract:

- `headers` → seed Postman `request.header[]` (with secrets stripped per §8)
- `queryParams` → seed `request.url.query[]`
- `uriParams` → seed `request.url.variable[]`
- `requestPath` + `method` → seed URL path and `request.method`

### 6.5 Step 5 — Inline request body

Resolve any `readUrl('classpath://testdata/{suite}/business-inbound/...json')` reference to the actual file under `src/test/resources/testdata/{suite}/business-inbound/`. Inline its content into `request.body.raw`.

### 6.6 Step 6 — Populate the response example

Locate `<munit-tools:assert-equals>` / `<munit-tools:assert-that>` referencing `attributes.statusCode` or expected response payloads. Use this to:

- Set the response example name and `code` in the Postman `response[]` array.
- Populate `response[*].body` from the matching `business-outbound/*-response.json`.

### 6.7 Fallback chain

If a fixture is missing for a given endpoint × scenario:

1. Fall back to the RAML `examples/*.raml` for that endpoint.
2. If no RAML example exists, ASK the user via `AskQuestion` to paste a sample payload.
3. If the user skips, **do not emit a placeholder body** — drop the request and surface a chat note.

### 6.8 Per-Endpoint, Per-Use-Case Tailoring

Emitted negative scenarios must be derived **strictly from what exists** for each endpoint, never blanket-applied:

- For each endpoint discovered in the RAML, build the negative set as the **intersection** of:
  - (a) the four-code allow-list `{400, 401, 404, 500}`, and
  - (b) the codes that have a corresponding MUnit test or RAML `responses:` entry for *that specific endpoint*.
- If an endpoint has zero negative tests, **only the happy path is emitted** for it. Do not invent negatives.
- If an endpoint has only `400` and `404` evidenced, only those two negative requests are emitted — `401` and `500` are skipped for that endpoint.

### 6.9 Deduplication

If multiple MUnit tests cover the same `endpoint × scenario`, emit **one** Postman request, not one per test. Choose the test whose fixture has the most complete payload (largest non-empty `business-inbound` JSON).

## 7. Postman Collection Schema

The output must be a Postman v2.1 collection. Use this skeleton (a tightened version of `agents/postman/examples/sfmc-consents-sys-api.postman_collection.json` if present):

```json
{
  "info": {
    "name": "{api-name}",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json",
    "description": "Auto-generated from RAML + MUnit. Set anypoint_client_id and anypoint_client_secret in the Variables tab before running."
  },
  "variable": [
    { "key": "base_url_local", "value": "http://0.0.0.0:8081" },
    { "key": "base_url_DEV",   "value": "<resolved-or-placeholder>" },
    { "key": "base_url_QA",    "value": "<resolved-or-placeholder>" },
    { "key": "base_url_UAT",   "value": "<resolved-or-placeholder>" },
    { "key": "base_url_PROD",  "value": "<resolved-or-placeholder>" },
    { "key": "anypoint_client_id",     "value": "", "type": "string" },
    { "key": "anypoint_client_secret", "value": "", "type": "secret" },
    { "key": "sourceSystem", "value": "<from-user>" },
    { "key": "targetSystem", "value": "<from-user>" }
  ],
  "item": [
    {
      "name": "DEV",
      "item": [
        {
          "name": "Happy Path",
          "item": [
            /* one request per endpoint, one per success status */
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "Negative — 400 / 401 / 404 / 500 (only those evidenced per endpoint)",
          "item": [
            /* one request per (endpoint × negative scenario actually present in MUnit/RAML) */
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
    /* additional env folders (QA, UAT, PROD, local) follow the same shape */
  ]
}
```

### 7.1 Per-Request Shape

Every request emitted must conform to:

```json
{
  "name": "{METHOD} {path} — {scenario}",
  "request": {
    "auth": { "type": "noauth" },
    "method": "POST",
    "header": [
      { "key": "client_id",        "value": "{{anypoint_client_id}}",     "type": "text" },
      { "key": "client_secret",    "value": "{{anypoint_client_secret}}", "type": "text" },
      { "key": "x-correlation-id", "value": "{{$guid}}",                  "type": "text" },
      { "key": "sourceSystem",     "value": "{{sourceSystem}}",            "type": "text" },
      { "key": "targetSystem",     "value": "{{targetSystem}}",            "type": "text" },
      { "key": "Content-Type",     "value": "application/json",            "type": "text" }
    ],
    "body": {
      "mode": "raw",
      "raw": "<inlined business-inbound JSON>",
      "options": { "raw": { "language": "json" } }
    },
    "url": {
      "raw": "{{base_url_DEV}}/api/v1/{path}",
      "host": [ "{{base_url_DEV}}" ],
      "path": [ "api", "v1", "{path-segment}" ],
      "query": [ /* from <munit:set-event> queryParams */ ],
      "variable": [ /* from <munit:set-event> uriParams */ ]
    }
  },
  "response": [
    {
      "name": "{scenario} — {statusCode}",
      "originalRequest": { /* mirror of request */ },
      "code": 200,
      "status": "OK",
      "_postman_previewlanguage": "json",
      "header": [ { "key": "Content-Type", "value": "application/json" } ],
      "body": "<inlined business-outbound JSON>"
    }
  ]
}
```

### 7.2 Path & Naming

- Request `name` format: `{METHOD} {path} — {scenario}` — e.g., `POST /api/v1/consents/opt-ins — Happy Path`, `POST /api/v1/consents/opt-ins — 400 Validation Error`.
- Folder structure: `<ENV>` → `Happy Path` | `Negative — ...` → individual requests.
- One Postman request per `endpoint × scenario`, per env.

## 8. Security Rules (Hard Checklist)

Verify all items before writing the file. **Abort generation if any check fails.**

- `client_id` and `client_secret` headers ALWAYS reference `{{anypoint_client_id}}` / `{{anypoint_client_secret}}`. **Never** copy literal values from MUnit fixtures, properties files, or the reference Postman collection (which contains hardcoded secrets and must NOT be replicated verbatim).
- Bearer tokens use `{{bearer_token}}` only. Never inline a real JWT.
- The collection-level `variable[]` for `anypoint_client_secret` uses `"type": "secret"` so Postman masks it in the UI.
- **Pre-write secret scan:** scan the generated JSON for any value matching the regex `[A-Za-z0-9_\-]{16,}` adjacent to a key matching `client_secret|password|api_key|api[_-]?token|access[_-]?token|bearer|token` (case-insensitive). If any non-template value is found, abort and report the offending location.
- Do not include OAuth `account_id`, real `access_token`, or any signed JWT pulled from MUnit fixtures.
- Mask obvious PII from MUnit `business-inbound` fixtures the same way `review-logging-standards.mdc` masks payloads — phone numbers (`17742581354` → `1XXXXXXXXXX`), emails (`user@example.com` → `u***@example.com`), account IDs.

### 8.1 Anti-Pattern Reference

The reference collection `agents/postman/examples/sfmc-consents-sys-api.postman_collection.json` (if present) contains hardcoded credentials at lines 20, 52, 167, and 172. These are **anti-patterns**. Use the file for **structural** reference only — never copy header values verbatim.

## 9. Standard Header Set

Every business request must include these headers (referenced via collection variables, never literals):

- `client_id: {{anypoint_client_id}}`
- `client_secret: {{anypoint_client_secret}}`
- `x-correlation-id: {{$guid}}` (Postman dynamic variable, randomized per send)
- `sourceSystem: {{sourceSystem}}`
- `targetSystem: {{targetSystem}}`
- `Content-Type: application/json` (only when a request body is present)

Health endpoints (`/ping`, `/health-check`) get only `x-correlation-id`.

## 10. Output Location & Validation

### 10.1 Output Path

Primary: `agents/postman/examples/{api-name}.postman_collection.json`.

If a Mule project root is detected (via `pom.xml` + `src/main/mule/`), also offer to write a copy to `<project-root>/postman/{api-name}.postman_collection.json`. Ask the user before writing the second copy.

### 10.2 Post-Generation Validation Checks

Run these checks after building the JSON, before writing:

- `info.schema` is exactly `https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json`.
- Every `request.url.raw` parses to a valid URL whose `host[]`/`path[]` arrays match the `raw` string (no orphaned segments).
- The §8 secret scan finds zero non-template values.
- At least one happy-path request exists per HTTP endpoint (negatives are only required where evidenced — see §6.8).
- **Status-code allow-list:** every emitted negative request has `response[*].code` in `{400, 401, 404, 500}`. Any other code (`403`, `409`, `422`, `502`, `503`, `504`, timeouts, etc.) anywhere in the file fails validation.
- **No event-driven flows:** the file contains no requests derived from MQ/scheduler/file/JMS-triggered flows (those are pre-filtered in §6.2).
- Every request references `{{base_url_<ENV>}}` for its host — no literal CloudHub domains in `request.url.host[]`.

If any check fails, fix the issue and re-run validation. Do not write a non-conformant file.

## 11. Anti-Patterns (DO NOT)

- **DO NOT** hardcode `client_id` / `client_secret` / bearer tokens.
- **DO NOT** replicate `Bearer eyJ...` JWTs from MUnit fixtures or other collections.
- **DO NOT** emit any status code outside the strict allow-list `{400, 401, 404, 500}` for negatives — including `403`, `409`, `422`, `502`, `503`, `504`, and timeouts.
- **DO NOT** blanket-apply all four negative codes to every endpoint — emit **only the codes evidenced per endpoint** (§6.8).
- **DO NOT** generate Postman requests for **non-HTTP-triggered flows** — flows whose source is `anypoint-mq:subscriber`, `scheduler`, `file:listener`, `jms:listener`, `vm:listener`, or any other event-driven source must be skipped entirely (§6.2).
- **DO NOT** embed real account IDs, tenant IDs, or PII from MUnit `business-inbound` fixtures (mask per §8).
- **DO NOT** use `0.0.0.0` as a host outside the `local` env folder.
- **DO NOT** generate one-request-per-MUnit-test — deduplicate to one Postman request per `endpoint × scenario` (§6.9).
- **DO NOT** silently fail URL resolution — always follow the three-tier fallback in §5.

## 12. Reference Example

`agents/postman/examples/sfmc-consents-sys-api.postman_collection.json` (if present) is the **structural** reference. It demonstrates:

- v2.1 schema URL
- nested `item[]` folder structure
- `request.method` / `request.header[]` / `request.body.raw` / `request.url.{raw,protocol,host,port,path}` shape
